meta {
  name: Place the textbook pages on the topic
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/placements
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "curriculum_id": "{{curriculumId}}",
    "curriculum_node_id": "{{curriculumTopicId}}",
    "content_item_id": "{{libraryItemId}}",
    "grain": "node",
    "page_start": 12,
    "page_end": 28,
    "origin": "manual"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.grain: eq node
  res.body.status: eq proposed
  res.body.origin: eq manual
  res.body.created_by: eq {{callerPersonId}}
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("placementId", res.body.id);
}

docs {
  Pages 12 to 28 of the prescribed book are where prime factorisation is
  taught. That sentence is the whole point of this row: without it a search
  about factor trees can only return "somewhere in this 300 page book".

  Both ends are checked before the row is written. The curriculum must be one
  the caller may write, the node must belong to that curriculum, the content
  item must be one the caller may read, and a content_node_id, if sent, must
  belong to that item. Each failure is a 422 naming the field, because a
  placement that points at something the caller cannot see is either a mistake
  or a probe.

  created_by is taken from the token. Who claimed this mapping is half of what
  makes confirming it meaningful, so it is not the caller's to assert.

  Note the status: created proposed even though a person typed it. Confirming
  is a separate request on purpose -- see request 6.
}
